WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.083
STEPHANIE WONG: If you've
ever used a static route

00:00:02.083 --> 00:00:04.890
to define how traffic can
move between private networks,

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.690
then you probably
know how vulnerable

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.240
it is to things like
component failures,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.727
or the wind, or squirrels.

00:00:12.727 --> 00:00:14.310
In this episode of
"Cloud Networking,"

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.320
I'll show you how to
leverage cloud router

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.480
to create dynamic
routing and not

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.612
have to worry about cute,
fuzzy networking hazards.

00:00:21.612 --> 00:00:25.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.900
Imagine you have a VPC setup
with multiple VMs in a subnet.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.300
Each VM's traffic is directed
through static routes

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.060
to a cloud VPN gateway
encrypting traffic

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.460
to and from your
on-premise network.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.800
This is fine, as long as
you're on-prem network

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.650
has a firewall and router
which know how to send traffic

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.430
to your cloud VPN gateway.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.750
Now, what happens if you
have two networks on premise?

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.210
This would require
you to manually add

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.290
static routes in Google Cloud
that expand each VMs routing

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.650
table to include new
network paths on premise.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.780
And you need to
reconfigure your VPN

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.349
on both ends, which causes
it to drop [RASPBERRY]

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:05.760
and re-establish.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.800
This isn't exactly
fun, especially

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.860
for larger organizations who
turn up new testing networks

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.845
several times a week or more.

00:01:12.845 --> 00:01:14.220
Static routes in
this case can be

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.460
painful for a number of reasons.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.210
A topology change
on either network

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.940
requires you to manually
update static routes.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.700
Also, static routes can't
automatically reroute traffic

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.050
if a link fails.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.990
What we want is a solution where
our networks can automatically

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.560
and rapidly discover topology
changes and then route traffic

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.190
accordingly so our users
don't sense disruptions.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.960
This is exactly
what dynamic routing

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.210
does, which Google
Cloud Platform offers

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.820
as Cloud Router.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.020
Now, if a link
fails, Cloud Router

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.580
can automatically
reroute traffic

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.410
if another path is possible.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.682
It works like this.

00:01:47.682 --> 00:01:49.890
When you extend your on-prem
network to Google Cloud,

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.730
Cloud Router peers with your
on-prem VPN gateway or router.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.710
The routers exchange
topology information

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.920
through border
gateway protocol, BGP.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.160
Cloud Router advertises
subnets from its VPC network

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.740
to the on-premise
gateway via BGP.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.170
Then topology changes
automatically propagate

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.070
between your VPC
and on-prem network.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.060
A great side effect
can be lower latency

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.360
because Cloud Router learns the
on-premise routes through BGP

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.760
and lets the network
infrastructure pick the best

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.780
route to reach the destination.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.408
If you're using Cloud
Router with VPN,

00:02:21.408 --> 00:02:22.950
it frees you from
management headache

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.720
for a number of reasons.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.850
You don't need to
statically manage subnets,

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:30.190
like adding and removing
static routes for your VPN.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.370
It scales with your
traffic because it's not

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.400
a physical device that
might cause a bottleneck.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.950
And the BGP session informs
each router of local changes,

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.160
meaning any new subnets
you add are seamlessly

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.840
advertised between networks.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.820
No downtime or manual
changes to routing tables.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.450
Let's walk through
Cloud Router setup.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.260
If you remember from
my last episode,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.410
I had a static route VPN setup.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:56.130
Let's create a dynamic router
for this VPN setup in US East

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.220
by going to the
Cloud Router page.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.000
Here, I'll give it a name.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.990
Select the VPC network
that contains the instances

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.640
that you want to reach.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.580
Choose a region where you want
to locate the Cloud Router.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.370
It advertises all subnets in
the region where it's located.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.350
Pick a Google ASN
you're not using

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.080
anywhere else in your network.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.130
An ASN uniquely
identifies each network

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.130
on the internet
for BGP sessions.

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.010
Choose whether to advertise all
subnets visible to the Cloud

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.860
Router or create custom routes.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.320
Next, you can add a
tunnel to each VPN gateway

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.470
and establish BGP sessions
between the two cloud routers

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:35.080
or with your on-premise
router, which you can check out

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.300
in the documentation below.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.550
Cloud Router is the
orchestral conductor

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.250
for traffic between your
on-prem and the cloud,

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:45.172
like when using VPN
and Cloud Interconnect.

00:03:45.172 --> 00:03:46.630
It helps a growing
network continue

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.050
to function in the face of
failures and other furry

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:53.470
rodents to support
scalability and adaptability.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:54.640
Stay tuned for more.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.620
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.540
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:02.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

